 ### Broad flower-colour geography is a biological template, not a single environmental mechanism
 
-The first answer to the flower-colour puzzle is that the polymorphism is strongly geographical. Both whether pigmentation was expressed and how intense already-pigmented flowers became covaried with broad environmental structure, while continuous residual spatial fields remained on scales of tens to more than 100 km. Previous work has shown that floral pigmentation and reflectance can track climatic gradients and colonization history (Koski & Ashman, 2015; Koski & Galloway, 2020); our results extend that logic by showing that the white–pigmented transition and within-pigmented intensity should not automatically be treated as one ecological axis. Temperature covaried with both components, whereas the additional topographic association was confined to intensity. The data therefore suggest at least two layers of geographical regulation: processes controlling whether a visible pigment state is maintained, and processes modulating colour after pigmentation is present.
-
-We do not assign the temperature or topographic associations to a single physiological mechanism. The photographs are not pigment assays, environmental axes combine correlated variables and the spatial field can absorb unmeasured climate, dispersal history and population structure. Ecologically, the important result is that broad geography provides a strong baseline against which local hypotheses must be judged. That baseline also explains why simply adding a national pollinator SDM to the same regression would be unsatisfactory: a pollinator surface can inherit much of the same geography.
+The clearest abiotic result was thermal, not a generic statement that “environment mattered.” Pigmented flowers were less frequent toward warmer maximum/growing-season climates, and flowers that remained pigmented were also paler. This matches the predefined thermal-regulation hypothesis and is biologically compatible with experimental evidence that petal anthocyanin accumulation and pathway-gene expression can increase at lower temperature and be suppressed by prolonged heat (Naing et al., 2018; Zhou et al., 2021). It also agrees with natural-season evidence that temperature can alter floral anthocyanin flux and transcription during flower development (Lu et al., 2009). The same environmental axis acting on both response parts suggests that temperature may influence both crossing the visible-pigmentation threshold and the amount or optical expression of colour above that threshold.
+
+That agreement does not identify adaptation or plasticity by itself. The photographs are not pigment assays, Temperature PC1 is a broad climatological axis rather than flower-level temperature, and geographically structured genotypes may differ in pathway regulation. The negative association could therefore reflect environmentally induced expression, local adaptation, population history correlated with climate or some combination. Common-garden temperature treatments paired with petal spectroscopy, anthocyanin chemistry and expression of structural/regulatory genes would distinguish these mechanisms directly.
+
+The other abiotic predictions were not supported in the same way. Shortwave radiation, precipitation/moisture, climatic seasonality and both soil axes had credible intervals spanning zero after continuous space was fitted. Thus the national data do not support a simple rule that stronger irradiance, drier conditions or nutrient-poor soils independently produce darker flowers, despite plausible stress and photoprotection roles for flavonoids. The terrain result was also specific: conditional intensity decreased on steeper, rougher terrain. Because this axis measures slope, roughness and TRI rather than elevation itself, it should not be paraphrased as “high-elevation flowers are paler.” It may reflect terrain-mediated microclimate, exposure, resource limitation or demographic structure and requires site-level measurements before a mechanism is assigned.
+
+Residual space supplied a second biological result. The fitted range for pigmentation state was about twice that for conditional intensity (133 versus 61 km), consistent with the prediction that threshold-like expression states may retain broader regional organization while intensity varies over shorter environmental or population scales. Yet the range intervals overlapped, and the SPDE field can contain omitted climate, dispersal history and genetic structure. The defensible conclusion is therefore that pigmentation state and intensity carry different spatial signatures, not that the model has reconstructed a specific colonization history. This distinction makes the broad stage mechanistically useful: it identifies a temperature-linked physiological hypothesis and a separate unresolved regional component that later local analyses must not mistake for pollinator or human effects.
 
